00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:44.330
>> We have a consulting herpetologist.

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:48.090
So one of the things you do is anytime you work anywhere

00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:50.970
with federal money or state money,

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:55.610
you have to do a survey for something called the National Diversity

00:09:55.770 --> 00:09:56.250
Database.

00:09:56.905 --> 00:10:00.025
That's basically looking for everything endangered,

00:10:00.025 --> 00:10:00.825
threatened,

00:10:00.905 --> 00:10:01.785
unhappy.

00:10:02.505 --> 00:10:03.145
So

00:10:03.785 --> 00:10:05.145
surprising no one,

00:10:06.025 --> 00:10:08.425
that area is found to have a

00:10:08.745 --> 00:10:10.105
turtle population

00:10:10.265 --> 00:10:11.545
that is threatened.

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:16.080
It happens the Hop River, if you don't know, has one of the largest,

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:20.400
surviving populations of a particular turtle,

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:22.080
in New England.

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:23.600
So it's not

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:25.200
endangered,

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:28.595
but it's threatened. So it's one category down from,

00:10:28.755 --> 00:10:29.395
you know,

00:10:30.995 --> 00:10:33.875
so basically anytime you do anything near there,

00:10:34.275 --> 00:10:37.635
you have to have a consulting herpetologist.

00:10:38.435 --> 00:10:43.190
And it turns out there's been a bat species identified in that area.

00:10:43.510 --> 00:10:50.710
So they're not requiring us to have a bat guru. I don't know what you call a batologist

00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:53.590
